    "title": "Forest management in Pennsylvania, USA",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Thinning, and the clearing of dense or dead material to lessen the danger of wildfire, likewise yields low-use wood. Yet keeping back a certain amount of what most would write off as low-use wood matters greatly. Stripping it away is partly a way of taking off the fuel that a fire would burn; but when a tree is felled, its top is best left where it falls, so that the minerals locked inside it seep back into the earth. Trunks pitted with hollows are, likewise, priceless as homes for the creatures that prey on insects — woodpeckers, bats and other small mammals — which keep troublesome insect numbers down and make the forest healthier. Nor is every small tree low-use: some, such as the rowan, are a ready source of food for birds and other animals. And any species that is uncommon in a given wood should be left to grow, for it adds to the forest's structural variety.",
