    "id": "64818abf8de50375de15bf68",
    "title": "Conquering Earth’s space junk problem",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "The number of objects aloft keeps growing even as our means of tracking them improve, and this has pushed firms, governments and other spacefarers into novel forms of cooperation against a shared danger. Bodies operating across borders — the Joint Orbital Debris Advisory Council among them — have put together recommendations aimed at protecting the long-term usability of orbital space. Under these, a satellite that has reached the end of its working life should be shut down, with any pressurised gas or leftover propellant that might trigger a blast vented off first. The same bodies also recommend easing retired craft far enough into the atmosphere for them to burn up within twenty years. Yet barely a third of all missions have honoured that twenty-year target, according to Tomasz Reinholt, who heads the orbital-debris office at the Pan-European Space Directorate in Vienna. Those planning the giant constellations insist they will behave as conscientious custodians of space, but Reinholt fears trouble may grow regardless of good intentions. 'And the ones that fold or go insolvent — what then?' he asks. 'They are hardly going to lay out money to pull their satellites back down.'",
