    "id": "648295d409c1857e1fd65637",
    "title": "The steam car",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "For all its brilliance, the Reyburns' steam car never made money. At something like 3,200 pounds in 1921 it lay within reach only of the very rich. Nor did it help that no two examples of the Mark V were ever quite alike, since Cornelius Reyburn could never stop adjusting the design. Fewer than forty of these remarkable cars had left the works by the time the firm closed in 1928. Reyburn himself never wavered: right up to his death in 1958 he insisted that steam was every bit the equal of petrol, and quite possibly better. The evidence rather supports him. Many of the surviving Mark Vs still run perfectly well, a few having covered more than half a million miles on nothing but routine servicing. Remarkably, an unaltered Mark V burns cleanly enough to meet the emission limits now enforced in London, rules with a reputation for being rigorous. The technology is not without its awkward problems, yet it is hard not to wonder what a steam car might achieve with modern materials and computers. With so much pressure nowadays to make vehicles perform better and pollute less, the return of the steam car is not so far-fetched an idea.",
