    "id": "6482cf78231b1a0a79786e90",
    "title": "Green roofs",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Across the world's cities it has become an ordinary sight: rooftops planted with turf, vegetable plots and dense greenery. Investment in these green roofs keeps climbing, from private firms and municipal authorities alike, drawn by the sheer range of returns — smaller sums paid out for cooling, a lower threat of flooding, fresh habitat for urban wildlife, cleaner air, and even the chance to raise crops several storeys up. Faced with competition for resources and shrinking open land, cities turn to such bold designs to adapt. What settles their success is not the technology alone but the readiness of councils, companies and other bodies to take part, backed by study of which solutions suit a city's varied roofs. Increasingly, planners also design green roofs to work alongside other environmentally minded features — 'living walls' clad in vegetation over soil or water, and street-level drainage such as lines of trees — so that rainfall is managed better and the built landscape grows more sustainable. Ireland came late to all this, and its local authorities are largely the ones driving it along; Dublin, where a far-sighted measure called the City Canopy Scheme has more than doubled the green-roof area, holds much of the national market.",
