    "id": "666ff1d283f7c5760d7242bd",
    "title": "The pirates of the ancient Mediterranean",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "The earliest surviving mention of piracy in the Mediterranean belongs to the kingdom of Amuria, in the reign of King Hadrimel (c 1400–1360 BCE), though it must surely reach back well before that kingdom rose — partly because the sea counted for so much to the surrounding peoples, partly because of the lie of the land. Much of the coast is good farmland, yet parts are broken, steep, in places mountainous, and the communities scratching a living in those harsher spots leaned heavily on whatever the sea could offer, fish and salt included. Almost all owned boats, handled them with skill, and knew the shoreline and its sea-lanes as no outsider could; small wonder that in lean times such men took to raiding. The coast favoured them, too, its many hidden inlets offering places to conceal a boat and strike without warning. No vessel of the day could yet cross open water for any distance — the nimble caravel* lay centuries in the future — so nearly every ship kept to a few familiar lanes along the shore. A slow, cargo-heavy merchantman cornered on one of them could do little but surrender, and by the time a state's warships arrived, the raiders' knowledge of the coast had let them vanish. * caravel: a small, highly manoeuvrable sailing ship, developed in Portugal in the fifteenth century",
