    "title": "The Step Pyramid of Djoser",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Well beneath the pyramid's base lay the burial chamber in which the king was interred, encircled by a bewildering web of tunnels off which small rooms opened, the whole arrangement designed to baffle intruders. Among the more puzzling things the pyramid gave up was an immense hoard of stone jars. In storerooms leading off the underground galleries, upwards of 30,000 of them came to light, in every imaginable size and shape. Fashioned from many kinds of stone, they bear the names of rulers from Egypt's earliest dynasties. On why they had been placed in Djoser's tomb, and what they were meant to signify, specialists have never reached agreement. Ludwig Steinmann, who cleared the greater part of the pyramid and its complex, took the view that they had first been kept in storage and were later reburied with due ceremony by Djoser as a tribute to the kings who came before him. A rival explanation, favoured by other scholars, is that the jars were simply tipped down the shafts in one further bid to keep robbers away from the royal chamber.",
