    "id": "66710b817f13441581c4b415",
    "title": "The global importance of wetlands",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "The trouble begins the moment logging crews move in to fell the trees. They cut channels through the swamp so boats can get in and float the trunks back out along the same routes; these cuts then draw the water off the peatland, clearing the ground for corn, oil palms or pulpwood. With the water gone, bacteria and fungi attack the carbon locked in the peat, giving off carbon dioxide together with methane. The solid remainder, meanwhile, begins to sink – a slow settling known as subsidence – and since peat is around nine-tenths water, this ranks among the most worrying results of draining it. ‘In the tropics the surface drops by roughly three centimetres a year, so within fifty years vast areas of the Congo Basin and coastal New Guinea will sit below the water and flood,’ Brandt warns. ‘A disaster is being assembled; some regions stand to lose as much as a third of their land.’",
