    "id": "667143a7a048d9678566e914",
    "title": "Deep-sea mining",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Geological surveys suggest that the seabed holds greater quantities of nickel, cobalt and rare-earth elements than every land deposit put together. Companies hoping to mine it argue that reaching for these underwater reserves would broaden the sources from which such metals are drawn, and they stress how sharply demand is climbing — for copper, for aluminium, and above all for the lithium and cobalt that electric-vehicle batteries and consumer electronics consume in ever greater volumes. Ore hauled from the ocean floor, they claim, would be richer than anything dug on land and would leave behind almost no residue. Techniques vary, but nearly all of them adapt equipment once built for mining on land: material is cut loose thousands of metres down, a slurry of rock and seawater is pumped up to waiting ships, ‘de-watered’, and passed to a second vessel, while the drained seawater is sent back to the depths and released near the seabed.",
