    "id": "68641ff85ce49e4ad14ddddf",
    "title": "Adapting to the effects of climate change",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "For many coastal settlements a sea wall is the obvious answer, yet along the soft, silt-laden northern coast of Larang such walls give way time and again, only deepening the erosion they were built to halt. The mangroves of that coast have been the target of one restoration attempt after another; these are tangled stands of trees and shrubs whose dense, mesh-like roots guard the shore by catching sediment, lifting the seabed and draining the strength from waves and tides. According to Bram Okonkwo of the Coastal Wetlands Alliance, most such replanting drives, popular though they are, come to nothing. His organisation opted instead for another route, erecting semi-permeable barriers of bamboo and brushwood that stand in for mangrove roots and coax the trees into re-establishing themselves unaided. Success has been modest, and greatest where the ground is subsiding least. The weakness of conventional infrastructure, Okonkwo argues, is its habit of chasing one fix at a time; lasting results demand a move toward many-sided methods that fold in natural processes while drawing in and rewarding the communities and local decision-makers on the ground.",
