    "title": "Palm oil",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Bello's caution is borne out even by the charge of deforestation, which turns out to be less clear-cut than it first appears. A hectare of oil palm yields at least four times — and possibly as much as ten times — the oil that the same area of soya, rapeseed or sunflower would give. That remarkable productivity, the very thing that makes the crop so profitable, may also count in its favour ecologically: if a plot of oil palm delivers ten times the oil of a rival crop, then ten times the land would have to be stripped bare to match its output using that rival. The carbon question, too, hangs on what the oil palm has replaced. Plants differ in how much carbon they lock away from the air and hold in their tissue, and the more a crop stores, the more it blunts the warming of the climate. 'In certain respects,' Bello notes, 'growing oil palm actually captures more carbon than some of the alternatives. Clear virgin forest for it, as has been allowed to happen unchecked in Indonesia and Malaysia, and the result is disastrous. But put it where rice once grew, and it may well store more carbon than before.'",
