    "id": "62babbe9e7d3741a8cfd2b28",
    "title": "Does education fuel economic growth?",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "On top of a full demographic reconstruction of everyone who lived in the two communities, Marogg and her colleagues have built this immense record of possessions. 'We can follow the very same people - and the offspring who came after them - right across three hundred years of educational and economic change,' she says. Whole lives have risen up out of the documents. Take the cousins Barbla and Ursina Cadonau, both twenty-two, who in 1698 were scolded for poring over printed books in church instead of attending to the preacher. 'That tells us they were still sharpening their reading a good decade after their schooldays had ended,' Marogg says. Or take Anna Barbla Tscharner, a forty-eight-year-old unmarried woman from the mountain village of Tarnau, whom the linen-weavers' guild reprimanded in 1738 for spinning and finishing cloth against its statutes. When she went on taking work reserved for the guild's male members, she was summoned before the guild court and ordered to pay a penalty worth a third of a maidservant's yearly wage. By today's standards it was a slight act of defiance, yet it belongs to an age when statutes in the German-speaking lands and elsewhere governed who could enter the labour market. The guilds' hold did more than keep people from using their skills; by barring outsiders from each trade, it smothered even the simplest industrial innovation.",
