    "title": "Timur Gareyev - blindfold chess champion",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "The scientists began with a battery of standard recall tests, gauging how well Gareyev could keep numbers, images and words in mind. In one familiar exercise, the subject hears a run of digits and must repeat them back, both in the given order and in reverse; seven or so is the usual limit. \"On none of these ordinary measures did he stand out,\" Voss noted. \"Aside from chess itself, we found nothing he was outstandingly good at.\" The scans, though, told a different story. With Gareyev lying inside the scanner, Voss examined how tightly his brain regions were linked, and the images revealed far more signalling than is typical between the parts that make up what is termed the frontoparietal control network. Among the 58 other people scanned for comparison, only a single individual rated more highly. \"That network comes into play in almost any demanding task,\" Voss said. \"It lets you direct your attention, hold the rules in your head, and judge when to act and when to hold back.\"",
