    "title": "Why we need silence",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Luka Boone, who researches psychotherapy, accepts that silence does not suit everyone — least of all those already wound up by stress. ‘Someone in that state often cannot settle or unwind in a silent setting,’ he notes, adding that a guide can help such people ease into silence by degrees until its rewards begin to show. All the same, Boone is sure that silence is far easier to come by in ordinary life than most of us assume. For one thing, it need not be total: in a study of his, people who sat outdoors in a quiet garden reported feeling noticeably calmer and less bored than others who were left alone in a room of complete silence. For another, a little goes a long way. ‘Hours of it are not the point,’ he says. ‘Short spells of quiet, taken often, do more good than one long stretch once a week. Simply tracking down the corners of your day where a bit of silence is available can make a real difference.’",
