    "id": "6054ab8b6ce06550b23a2a68",
    "title": "Why Being Bored Is Stimulating – And Useful, Too",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Why the attention system should break down in the first place is what Voss's group is now investigating. The research is at an early stage, but they suspect that personality accounts for at least part of it. A proneness to boredom has been tied to a range of traits. Those whose behaviour is driven chiefly by the pursuit of reward appear to suffer worst of all, whereas a quality such as inquisitiveness seems to lift a person's threshold for it. Further signs that boredom does real damage come from comparing people who are easily bored with those who are not: the former tend to fare worse in their schooling, their working lives and their fortunes generally. Boredom, admittedly, does not kill anyone directly — the danger lies in what we do to relieve it. So how might it be eased before matters reach that point? Brandt's team offers one answer. In work with adolescents, they found that youngsters who 'approach' a tedious situation — recognising it as dull and throwing themselves into it regardless — reported less boredom than those who reached instead for snacks, television or their social-media feeds.",
