    "id": "6054c4076ce06550b23a2a6f",
    "title": "Whatever Happened To The Harappan Civilisation?",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "‘We can trace the rise of the Harappans in a good deal of archaeological detail, but their decline is a different matter — the evidence for it is surprisingly thin,’ says Dr Halvard Brandt, an archaeologist at Ravensholt University. Numerous explanations have been floated over the years. Some researchers propose that great glacier-fed rivers altered their courses, throwing water supply and farming into disarray; others that swelling populations outgrew what the land could yield, or that the trade networks on which the cities leaned simply unravelled, or that warfare and invasion brought them low; still others point to a change in climate that upset the supply of food and water. ‘A single cause is unlikely,’ Brandt cautions. ‘The trouble is that, until recently, we have had very little hard evidence for any of these ideas. Much of the debate has amounted to well-argued guesswork.’",
