    "title": "UK companies need more effective boards of directors",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "A bolder answer, suited perhaps to a handful of very large firms whose operations are sprawling and intricate, is the professional board, on which members might serve three or four days a week and enjoy the backing of their own dedicated staff and advisers. A structure of that sort carries obvious dangers, and firm ground rules would be needed to stop it from treading on management's territory by involving itself in the daily conduct of the business. Questions of how to recruit such directors, how to reward them and how to preserve their independence might well surface, and the arrangement would hardly suit every company. Even so, boards that were more professional and far better briefed would have served the banks especially well, for there the salaried executives commanded a depth of inside knowledge their part-time colleagues could never match — a gulf that left those non-executives unable to grasp or foresee the 2008 crash.",
