    "id": "6056e2c9511ef10dfd387c93",
    "title": "The Desolenator: Producing Clean Water",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Each day the Desolenator can turn out around 20 litres of drinking water, which is plenty to keep a household supplied for cooking and drinking. Where it parts company with conventional desalination is in its power source: no mains electricity is called for, only sunshine. The unit is 110 cm by 80 cm, and a folding handle built into it means a single person can shift it from one spot to another with ease. Water is fed in along a pipe and then travels as a thin layer between a pane of double glazing and the top of a solar panel, which the sun warms. From there the heated water passes into a compact boiler — powered by a solar-charged battery — and is boiled off into steam; once that steam cools, what remains is distilled water. A basic mesh catches any particles, and it need only be shaken to clear them away. Two outlet pipes carry the liquid off: one takes the drinking water, while the other removes the residue — the salt extracted from seawater, fluoride and the like. The machine's condition is displayed on an LCD screen and relayed to the company, so that maintenance can be arranged whenever it is required.",
