    "id": "6056e69f511ef10dfd387c94",
    "title": "Why fairy tales are really scary tales",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "One common explanation is that tales of this sort survive because they preserve useful rules. A listener may learn not to disobey a host, accept unidentified food or cross a forbidden boundary. The difficulty is that the directions vary sharply. One version rewards silence, another requires shouting for help, while a third makes delay rather than disobedience the fatal mistake. Preliminary studies of repeated retellings also indicated that practical advice was not unusually durable. Explanations based on its usefulness had nevertheless been proposed from historical collections whose evidential value was uncertain, since editors often simplified language, removed repetitions or made separate versions resemble one another. To examine transmission without this interference, researchers turned to a controlled method derived from studies of memory and communication.",
