    "title": "The Development of Museums",
    "total_questions": 16,
    "passage": "Of late, though, the way we think about the past, and about how it ought to be shown to us, has shifted. Nowadays the governing term in heritage display is experience: the livelier the better, and ideally it should call on every sense. Britain offers fine instances, among them the Ceredig Living History Centre at Elming, the National Gallery of Sound and Moving Image at Harwick, and the Museum of the Wars at Dunmarsh. Across the Atlantic the tendency had surfaced far sooner, and the reconstructed settlement at Fairhaven became a model copied by heritage schemes the world over. Where all this ends, nobody can say. Acting out past events has caught on more and more at these so-called heritage locations, and before long computers will generate virtual-reality encounters that conjure up, in sharp detail, whatever era a visitor chooses, letting that visitor move through it as though part of the scene. Purists have condemned such ventures as an unbearable cheapening of the past, yet the popularity of so many historical theme parks and comparable attractions implies that most people do not agree.",
