    "id": "6059b77c511ef10dfd387cf2",
    "title": "The Context, Meaning and Scope of Tourism",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "Both economically and socially, tourism now carries far more weight than once it did. Across most industrialised nations it is the service sector that has grown fastest in recent years, and among the very largest components of that sector — though several of these countries still fail to treat it as a distinct activity — stands travel and tourism. The Global Tourism Alliance (1993) called it the biggest industry on earth by almost any economic yardstick, whether one weighs added value, investment, jobs or the taxes it yields. Total output, the Alliance reckoned, ran to some $4 trillion, more than 13 per cent of everything the world's consumers spend. Nowhere is there a larger employer: roughly 140 million people, close to 7 per cent of the global workforce, owe their living to it. As a contributor to output it likewise outstrips every rival, generating above 6 per cent of the world's product and drawing yearly capital investment beyond $450 billion, quite apart from the direct, indirect and personal taxes it raises. Through the broadening effect that travelling has on those who do it, and through the work it brings into being, tourism thus leaves a deep imprint not merely on the global economy but on society at large.",
