    "id": "60599241511ef10dfd387cda",
    "title": "Reducing the Effects of Climate Change",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "Several researchers have floated the notion of spraying aerosols into the stratosphere over the Arctic. Sulphur or hydrogen-sulphide aerosols would be used, the resulting sulphur dioxide forming a reflective haze that dims the incoming light worldwide. Nature supplies the model: great volcanic eruptions, Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines in 1991 among them, cooled the globe briefly by half a degree Celsius. A different line of enquiry has examined whether Greenland's ice sheets might be held in place by strong reinforced chains, so that icebergs are stopped from slipping out to sea. In the Russian Arctic, meanwhile, one plan calls for millions of birch trees to be planted. The evergreen pines native to the area shade the snow and soak up radiation; birches, by contrast, drop their leaves once winter comes, and the bare canopy lets the snow throw the radiation back. Diverting the great northern waterways to push more cold water toward the places where ice forms is another option some climate scientists put forward.",
