    "id": "605998a8511ef10dfd387cdd",
    "title": "What destroyed the civilisation of Easter Island?",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Scattered across Easter Island (Rapa Nui to those who live there) stand several hundred colossal stone figures, the moai. Polynesian voyagers reached this isolated speck in the Pacific and were then cut off from the wider world for centuries. Everything the moai demanded — and some rise more than nine metres and weigh over 6,000 kilos — came from the island's own resources. Yet the Spanish sailors who landed in 1740 found a people still living, technologically, in the Stone Age. Shaped with stone tools alone, the figures had somehow been carried many kilometres to the massive stone platforms on which they stood, without draught animals or wheels. Just who made them stayed unresolved deep into the twentieth century. The Danish anthropologist Aksel Vendt held that seafarers from the South American mainland were responsible, while the popular writer Lucien Berne credited survivors of a drowned mid-ocean continent. Linguistic, archaeological and genetic findings have since settled the matter — the builders were the islanders' own ancestors — though how they shifted such weights remains unproven. Island tradition insists the figures simply strode into place; scholars have more often assumed they were dragged with ropes and wooden rollers.",
