    "title": "The Lost City",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Ever since Vellacott's time, a single question has puzzled tourists, historians and archaeologists alike: why the site appears to have been left empty even before the Almiran Conquest. No Almiran chronicle refers to it at all, and had those writers had any idea that such a place stood so close to Tambara, they would certainly have come looking for gold. The view that has gained wide acceptance in recent years is that Sacyamarca was a sayana, a country estate raised by a Suri emperor as a refuge from the harsh winters of Tambara, somewhere the elite could enjoy grand architecture and magnificent views. What is more, the distinctive style of its buildings points to its being built in the reign of Rumaya (c. 1440–72), the greatest of all the Suri emperors. Custom dictated that each of Rumaya's descendants put up a similar estate of his own, and so Sacyamarca would have been abandoned after his death, roughly fifty years before the Almiran Conquest.",
