    "id": "60558f266ce06550b23a2a74",
    "title": "The Growth Of Bike-Sharing Schemes Around The World",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Eklöf's faith in bike-sharing never wavered, and midway through the 1990s two Finns approached him for help in getting a scheme going in Helsinki. What came of it was the first large-scale bike-share programme anywhere in the world. The system ran on a deposit: a coin slotted into the bike was handed back once the bike was returned. Having built the Finnish system, Eklöf resolved to have another go on home ground — and on this occasion he managed to catch the attention of Sweden's Ministry of Transport. 'The times were different by then,' he remembers. 'Environmental awareness had grown, and Helsinki had shown that bike-sharing could actually work.' So it was that a fresh Yellow Bicycle plan opened in Stockholm in 1998. Riding the bikes, however, no longer came free: each trip now cost one krona, paid for using a chip card that the Swedish bank Nordgiro had developed. The bikes Eklöf drew up for it were bright and solidly built, held in purpose-made racks that the chip card unlocked; at the outset there were 250 of them, shared across five stations.",
