    "id": "605599c66ce06550b23a2a79",
    "title": "The Concept Of Intelligence",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "The first is that the private beliefs people hold about intelligence govern how they size up their own minds and those of others. To make sense of the judgements individuals reach about ability, their own and other people's, their implicit theories are a sensible place to begin. Take a parent: it is that parent's private notion of how language matures that decides the age at which they start correcting a child's speech and, more broadly, the age at which the child is thought ready for one mental task or another. The same holds beyond the home. Anyone interviewing candidates for a job draws on such beliefs when choosing whom to appoint, and so does anyone picking the people they wish to befriend. Knowledge of implicit theories matters, in short, because these theories are put to work daily, wherever people form judgements about one another.",
