    "id": "60d45ba4fbd81c3fcad12d51",
    "title": "Climate change reveals ancient artefacts in Norway's glaciers",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "Halvorsen's team obtained radiocarbon dates for 178 of the objects and lined those dates up against the region's major turning points — spells of warming and cooling on the one hand, and on the other the social and economic changes of the era, such as the spread of farming and the widening of international trade in the run-up to the Viking Age. Certain stretches of time had left behind a wealth of objects, a sign that people had been busy in the mountains then; from other stretches there was little or nothing. The distribution of these periods was, to Halvorsen, the real surprise. Sørdalen's heights are forbidding, and in the coldest spells glaciers could seal off the upper passes and make travel there all but impossible, so one might expect people to have kept to lower ground during an episode like the sixth-century cold snap of roughly 540 to 600 CE. Instead, judging by how much they had left behind on the ice, hunters went on climbing into the mountains as often as ever. 'What is striking is that the finds seem to carry on right through that cold phase,' he says, 'which may mean that hunting in the mountains mattered more, not less, when harvests were failing in the cold.' A downturn in the Scandinavian climate would have ruined crops across the region, and the loss would have pushed more people to rely on what they could hunt.",
