    "title": "Mapungubwe",
    "total_questions": 13,
    "passage": "At its height, most likely in the early 13th century, Mapungubwe may have been home to some 4,000 inhabitants. Its chief, whom people called the king, is thought to have been the richest of all, holding more cattle and valuables than any of his subjects. He and his counsellors occupied a walled compound of stone dwellings set on the very highest ground the kingdom possessed: a natural sandstone hill rising roughly 25 metres and stretching about 120 metres from one end to the other. People had lived on this hill since the 10th century, and postholes cut into the rock reveal that a wooden palisade once ringed the entire compound. Everyone else was housed in mud and thatch dwellings on the flat land below, where a single stone building also stood. That lower settlement, called Tshiluvi, spreads across some 6 hectares (14.8 acres) and is older than anything raised on the hilltop.",
