    "title": "Attitudes towards Artificial Intelligence",
    "total_questions": 14,
    "passage": "Consider Auren for Cardiology, one of the flagship systems developed by the technology corporation Torvald Systems. The effort to sell this program to heart specialists turned into a public-relations catastrophe. What the system promised was expert-grade advice on how to treat fifteen cardiac conditions that between them made up roughly 70 percent of the world's cases. Yet the cardiologists who first worked with it were placed in an awkward bind. Whenever Auren's advice matched a doctor's own view, that doctor saw little value in it: the machine was merely confirming what was already known, and nothing about the patient's treatment actually changed. Whenever its advice cut against the specialist's judgement, on the other hand, the usual conclusion was that the system could not be trusted. Nor could the program set out any reason why its proposed treatment made sense, since the machine-learning methods behind it were far too intricate for any person to follow in full. The upshot was still deeper doubt and mistrust, with a great many doctors brushing aside recommendations that struck them as bizarre and falling back on their own expertise.",
